    <section class="mb-14 rounded-2xl border border-border bg-surface p-6 sm:p-8 dark:border-border-dark dark:bg-surface-dark-alt">
      <div class="mb-6">
        <span class="text-xs font-bold uppercase tracking-wider text-brand-600 dark:text-brand-400">Community Answers</span>
        <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-text dark:text-text-dark mt-1 mb-2">
          Frequently Asked Questions (from Hacker News & Reddit)
        </h2>
        <p class="text-sm text-text-muted dark:text-text-dark-muted m-0">
          Real questions asked by developers and privacy advocates in our live community discussions:
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="space-y-6">
        <div class="rounded-xl border border-border/70 bg-surface-alt/40 p-5 dark:border-border-dark/70 dark:bg-surface-dark/40">
          <h3 class="text-base font-bold text-text dark:text-text-dark m-0 mb-2">
            Q: "Why did you only test the Pixel 8? What about Samsung OneUI or iPhone?"
          </h3>
          <p class="text-sm text-text-muted dark:text-text-dark-muted leading-relaxed m-0">
            We started with the Google Pixel 8 because it represents Google's baseline reference implementation of Android and serves as the hardware gold standard for privacy-hardened operating systems like GrapheneOS. By testing stock Android against GrapheneOS on identical physical silicon, we eliminated hardware and modem variables entirely.
          </p>
          <p class="text-sm text-text-muted dark:text-text-dark-muted leading-relaxed mt-2 m-0">
            That said, the community demand for <strong>Samsung OneUI</strong> (which introduces Samsung's own cloud telemetry on top of Google's) and <strong>Apple iOS</strong> has been massive. Both devices are currently queued on our workbench under the exact same isolated 72-hour sniffer protocol for our Round 2 benchmark release.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div class="rounded-xl border border-border/70 bg-surface-alt/40 p-5 dark:border-border-dark/70 dark:bg-surface-dark/40">
          <h3 class="text-base font-bold text-text dark:text-text-dark m-0 mb-2">
            Q: "Can I just block all 42 endpoints in Pi-hole or AdGuard Home?"
          </h3>
          <p class="text-sm text-text-muted dark:text-text-dark-muted leading-relaxed m-0">
            You can download our 42-endpoint blocklist file above, but <strong>be very careful: aggressive domain blocking on stock Android causes breaking side effects</strong>:
          </p>
          <ul class="mt-2 space-y-1.5 text-xs sm:text-sm text-text-muted dark:text-text-dark-muted list-disc pl-5">
            <li><strong>Blocking <code class="text-xs bg-surface dark:bg-surface-dark px-1 py-0.5 rounded font-mono">checkin.gstatic.com</code>:</strong> Breaks Wi-Fi captive portal detection and Android NTP network time synchronization.</li>
            <li><strong>Blocking <code class="text-xs bg-surface dark:bg-surface-dark px-1 py-0.5 rounded font-mono">mtalk.google.com</code>:</strong> Severs Firebase Cloud Messaging (FCM). Push notifications for Signal, WhatsApp, delivery apps, and banking 2FA will stop arriving in the background.</li>
            <li><strong>Blocking <code class="text-xs bg-surface dark:bg-surface-dark px-1 py-0.5 rounded font-mono">device-provisioning.googleapis.com</code>:</strong> Can fail Play Integrity / SafetyNet attestation checks inside banking and security apps.</li>
          </ul>
          <p class="text-sm text-text-muted dark:text-text-dark-muted leading-relaxed mt-2 m-0">
            If you want to use the list, test it in an isolated client profile first, and keep <code class="text-xs bg-surface dark:bg-surface-dark px-1 py-0.5 rounded font-mono">mtalk.google.com</code> whitelisted if your daily workflow requires push notifications.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div class="rounded-xl border border-border/70 bg-surface-alt/40 p-5 dark:border-border-dark/70 dark:bg-surface-dark/40">
          <h3 class="text-base font-bold text-text dark:text-text-dark m-0 mb-2">
            Q: "How did you capture and analyze that many packets without alerting the OS?"
          </h3>
          <p class="text-sm text-text-muted dark:text-text-dark-muted leading-relaxed m-0">
            Many phone tracking studies install on-device VPNs or profiling apps. This introduces observer bias because Google Play Services can throttle or alter background behavior when internal metering is detected.
          </p>
          <p class="text-sm text-text-muted dark:text-text-dark-muted leading-relaxed mt-2 m-0">
            Our team used a <strong>zero-agent external hardware wiretap</strong>: the phones were connected to a dedicated physical Wi-Fi AP broadcast from an enterprise pfSense firewall on an isolated VLAN. All egress traffic was mirrored into Wireshark running on a separate host machine. We parsed the resulting pcap files using custom Python scripts (<code class="text-xs bg-surface dark:bg-surface-dark px-1 py-0.5 rounded font-mono">pyshark</code> and <code class="text-xs bg-surface dark:bg-surface-dark px-1 py-0.5 rounded font-mono">dpkt</code>) cross-referenced against Alphabet's published BGP ASN 15169 routing tables.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

